.TH V32C++ 1 "September 2026" "v32c++ 20260925-dev" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
v32c++ \- transpile a subset of C++ to Vircon32 C
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B v32c++
.RB [ \-o
.IR output.c ]
.RB [ \-c ]
.RB [ \-v ... ]
.RB [ \-I
.IR dir ]...
.RB [ \-x ]
.RB [ \-b ]
.RB [ \-g ]
.RB [ \-\-target=vircon32 | standard ]
.I input.cpp
.br
.B v32c++
.B \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B v32c++
translates a purpose-built subset of C++ \(em classes, single
inheritance, constructors and destructors, virtual functions and
dynamic dispatch, operator overloading, arrays, and the usual
statement/expression language \(em into plain C targeting the
.B Vircon32
fantasy console's own C compiler. The generated C is handed to that
compiler exactly the way hand-written Vircon32 C would be; no other
tool sits between
.B v32c++
and the target.
.PP
This follows the same strategy Bjarne Stroustrup's original
.B cfront
used for the first C++ compilers: translate to C first, and let an
existing, trusted C compiler do everything after that.
.PP
Templates, exceptions, RTTI, and multiple inheritance are not part of
the language
.B v32c++
accepts, and never will be \(em these are permanent design boundaries,
not gaps awaiting a future release. See
.B LANGUAGE NOTES
below, and the project's own README, for what
.I is
supported and what else is still in progress.
.PP
.B v32c++
produces no output at all on success unless a verbosity flag is given
(see
.BR \-v ,
below) \(em this matches the Vircon32 C compiler's and
.BR v32lua (1)'s
own behavior. A non-zero exit status, and a message on standard
error, indicate a genuine problem: a syntax error, a semantic error
(an undeclared type, an inaccessible member, an unresolvable call,
and so on), or a missing
.B main
function when one was required (see
.BR \-c ).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o " output.c"
Write the generated C to
.IR output.c ,
instead of the default. If
.B \-o
is not given at all, the output filename is derived from the input's
own name: its extension is replaced with
.IR .c ,
so
.I foo/bar.cpp
becomes
.IR foo/bar.c .
This also matches the Vircon32 C compiler's and
.BR v32lua (1)'s
own default naming.
.TP
.B \-c
Transpile without requiring a
.B main
function to exist in the input. Like a real C compiler's own
.B \-c
("compile only"): use this for a library or module fragment that
is not, on its own, a complete, standalone-compilable program.
Without
.BR \-c ,
an input with no
.B main
is treated as an error.
.TP
.B \-v
Increase verbosity. Stackable \(em repeat for more detail, either as
separate flags
.RI ( "\-v \-v \-v" )
or bundled
.RI ( \-vvv ).
No
.B \-v
at all (the default) produces no output on success at all.
.RS
.TP
.B "single \-v"
Print a one-line progress message as each internal stage begins:
lexing/parsing, semantic analysis, lowering, and code generation.
.TP
.B "\-vv"
Everything from a single
.BR \-v ,
plus explanatory comments sprinkled directly into the generated
.I .c
itself, at the points where this project's own C++-to-C transformation
is least obvious to someone reading the output \(em see
.B EXPLANATORY COMMENTS
below. Pure commentary: never changes what code is emitted, only
whether a comment explaining it is emitted alongside it.
.TP
.B "\-vvv"
Everything from
.BR \-vv ,
plus a full dump of the parsed AST, a semantic-analysis summary
(class layouts, vtables, resolved calls), and the fully-lowered form
of every method and function body \(em genuinely useful for following
along with what the tool understood about a given program and how it
transformed it, not just debugging output.
.RE
.TP
.BI \-I " dir\fR, " \-\-include= dir
Add
.I dir
to the search path for
.I .hpp
and
.I .cpp
includes (see
.B HEADERS AND C TYPES
below). May be repeated; directories are searched in command-line
order. A quoted
.B #include
looks in the including file's own directory first, then in each
.IR dir ;
an angle-bracket
.B #include
looks only in the
.I dir
list. Has no effect on
.I .h
includes, which always pass through to the generated C unchanged.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version, author, and project URL information, and exit
immediately without processing any input.
.TP
.B \-b
Transpile a Vircon32
.B BIOS
rather than an ordinary cartridge (see
.B BIOS MODE
below): the generated XML's
.B <rom>
element gets
.BR type=\(dqbios\(dq ,
and three additional constraints are enforced, checked only under
.BR \-b :
exactly one
.B #texture
hint, at most one
.B #sound
hint, and a
.B "void error_handler()"
function present alongside
.BR main .
.TP
.B \-g
Also write
.IR <output>.debug ,
a sparse C-line/C++-line mapping (see
.B DEBUG MAPS
below) alongside the generated C. Not written unless
.B \-g
is given.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-no\-xml
Don't generate the Vircon32 cart-packing XML file (see
.B CART PACKAGING
below). Generated by default whenever a
.I .c
file is actually written; never generated at all under
.BR \-\-target=standard ,
with or without this flag.
.TP
.BI \-\-target= X
Output C dialect:
.B vircon32
(or
.BR v32 ,
the default) targets the real Vircon32 compiler's own quirks
throughout;
.B standard
(or
.BR std )
targets plain, portable C instead. No cart-packing XML or debug map
either way under
.BR \-\-target=standard ,
since both are Vircon32-platform concepts with no meaning outside it.
One real behavioral difference, not just a syntax swap:
.B main
's own return type \(em Vircon32 mode always forces
.IR "void main(void)" ,
discarding whatever the source declared; standard mode honors it
(typically
.IR int ),
preserving real
.B return
statements rather than stripping them. Vircon32 mode also rewrites
the ternary operator into an equivalent
.IR if / else
wherever it directly initializes a variable, is directly assigned to
a bare identifier, or is directly a
.B return
expression (including a chain of these) \(em the real Vircon32
compiler doesn't support the ternary operator at all; standard mode
keeps it exactly as written. Function pointers (including arrays of
them, and virtual-dispatch vtables) also produce correct standard-C
declarator/cast syntax under
.BR \-\-target=standard ,
not just Vircon32's own reversed form. See
.I docs/VIRCON32_QUIRKS.md
for the itemized list of everything this flag actually changes.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The input was transpiled successfully.
.TP
.B 1
A syntax error, a semantic error, a missing
.B main
function (without
.BR \-c ),
or an I/O error (the input could not be opened, or the output could
not be written) occurred. Details are printed to standard error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Transpile a single file, writing
.IR sprite.c :
.PP
.RS
.nf
v32c++ sprite.cpp
.fi
.RE
.PP
Transpile a library fragment with no
.B main
of its own, to a specific output path, watching each stage run:
.PP
.RS
.nf
v32c++ \-c \-v \-o build/widgets.c src/widgets.cpp
.fi
.RE
.PP
Inspect exactly what the tool understood about a program, without
writing an output file anywhere the caller didn't ask for:
.PP
.RS
.nf
v32c++ \-vvv sprite.cpp
.fi
.RE
.PP
Build a game that uses the C++ API headers from a v32c++ checkout
.RI ( "#include <v32/video.hpp>" ):
.PP
.RS
.nf
v32c++ \-I ~/src/v32c++ \-o build/game.c game.cpp
.fi
.RE
.PP
Produce portable C instead, for checking with a desktop compiler:
.PP
.RS
.nf
v32c++ \-\-target=standard \-o /tmp/game.c game.cpp
gcc \-std=c99 \-c /tmp/game.c
.fi
.RE
.SH LANGUAGE NOTES
This section is a brief summary, not a complete reference \(em see the
project's own README and
.I docs/DESIGN_NOTES.md
for the full, current picture, including known gaps under active
development.
.PP
Supported: classes and plain C-style
.BR struct s
alike (the only real difference \(em default member access: private for
.BR class ,
public for
.B struct
\(em is handled correctly; every other class feature is shared
identically between the two) with single inheritance and access
control; constructors and destructors, including automatic invocation
at scope exit and via
.BR delete ;
.B virtual
functions and dynamic dispatch, including virtual destructors;
operator overloading; function and constructor overload resolution;
.B new
and
.BR delete ,
including array forms; arrays, in either standard C declarator syntax
or Vircon32's own native style, accepted interchangeably as C++-side
input;
.I .hpp / .cpp
.B #include
resolution with
.B #pragma once
(every other
.B #
line is carried into the output verbatim; see
.B HEADERS AND C TYPES
below);
.B native
opaque type declarations for C types defined in pass-through headers
(see
.B HEADERS AND C TYPES
below);
inline assembly
.RI ( "asm { \(dq...\(dq }" ,
Vircon32's own form, passed through; GCC-style basic
.I asm(\(dq...\(dq)
and its
.BR __asm__ / volatile
spellings are accepted and re-emitted in Vircon32 form; extended asm
with operand constraints is rejected);
default parameter values;
.B friend
classes and functions;
.BR nullptr ;
direct-initialization with constructor arguments
.RI ( "Shape shape(7);" );
multiple declarators in one statement
.RI ( "int a, *b, c = 5;" );
function-pointer
.BR typedef s
in either declarator style;
.B break
and
.BR continue .
The two are correctly distinguished, not conflated:
.B break
is valid inside a
.B switch
alone (no enclosing loop needed), while
.B continue
is not \(em matching real C exactly. Real C
.B switch / case / default
fall-through semantics (no
.B break
means execution continues into the next label's own statements, the
same as real C); bitwise operators (
.BR & ", " | ", " ^ ", " << ", " >>
and their compound-assignment forms
.RI ( &= ", " |= ", " ^= ", " <<= ", " >>= ),
at C's own correct operator precedence \(em including the classic
.I "a & b == c"
gotcha (equality binds tighter than bitwise-and, so this parses as
.IR "a & (b == c)" ,
not
.IR "(a & b) == c" ),
handled exactly right rather than approximated;
C-style casts
.RI ( "(int)x" ", " "(Base *)ptr" )
and C++-style casts
.RI ( static_cast / const_cast / reinterpret_cast ,
all three transpiling identically to the C-style form \(em the
semantically correct simplification once the target is C, which has no
notion of any distinction between them);
hex/octal/binary integer literals
.RI ( "0x1F" ", " "013" ", " "0b1010" \(em
.B binary literals are a C++14 addition,
not part of every C++ standard, accepted here as a convenience
regardless) and integer/float literal suffixes
.RI ( "42u" ", " "100L" ", " "3.9f" ,
accepted and ignored \(em one integer type and one floating-point type
here, so a suffix has nothing left to disambiguate);
the ternary operator
.RI ( "cond ? a : b" )
at C's own correct precedence, between assignment and
.BR || ,
right-associative, so chaining nests the way real C++ does;
.BR do / while
(the body runs at least once, unconditionally, before the first check
\(em reuses the exact same loop-depth and destructor-boundary handling
as
.BR while / for ,
verified directly, not assumed);
.B enum
(top-level/namespace-level; auto-incrementing or explicit values,
resolved by the C compiler downstream exactly as real C++ would, not
computed by this project itself);
plain C-style
.B union
(top-level/namespace-level; NOT routed through the same machinery as
.BR class / struct ,
since real C++ itself restricts what a union may contain far more
than either);
source-level
.B sizeof
(both
.RI ( Type )
and
.I "expr"
/
.RI ( expr ),
with a call inside the expression form correctly resolved, not
skipped);
.B goto
and labeled statements (a real, deliberately-flagged limitation here
\(em see BUGS below);
function pointers, in BOTH Vircon32's own quirky declarator style
.RI ( "ReturnType(ParamTypes)* name;" )
and standard C's
.RI ( "ReturnType (*name)(ParamTypes);" ),
including arrays of either \(em output is always Vircon32's own
required form regardless of which the source used, the same dual-
acceptance treatment array declarators already have (see BUGS below
for one specific spelling that isn't yet confirmed against the real
compiler);
multi-dimensional arrays
.RI ( "int grid[8][4];" " or " "int [8][4] grid;" ,
dimensions nested outermost-first, matching real C exactly), with
chained subscripting
.RI ( "grid[i][j]" )
needing no new work at all;
.B const
as a type prefix everywhere a type can appear
.RI ( "const int x" ", " "const int *p" ,
a
.B const
parameter or return type) \(em resolving method calls and overloads
correctly through a const-qualified class type, not just parsing the
keyword \(em and as a trailing qualifier on member functions too
.RI ( "int getValue() const { ... }" ,
both declared in-class and defined out-of-line), which actually
propagates: the generated
.I this
parameter for a const method is
.RI ( "const ClassName *" ,
not a plain pointer with the qualifier silently dropped (see BUGS
below for the exact boundary on what this doesn't cover); a
reference-typed parameter's own call sites, which correctly insert the
implicit address-of a C++ reference argument needs once it lowers to a
plain C pointer
.RI ( "getArea(shape)" " becomes " "getArea__Shape_ref((&shape))" ,
including through a dereferenced pointer argument);
global (file-scope) variables;
base-class constructor delegation, both EXPLICIT (
.I ": Base(args)"
in a constructor's own member-initializer list) and IMPLICIT (no
.I ": Base(args)"
written at all still calls the base's own zero-argument constructor
automatically, matching real C++ \(em a base with no constructor of
its own needs nothing called; a base with some constructor but none
callable with zero arguments is a genuine, reported error, not a
silently uninitialized base subobject); primitive-typed
member-field initializers in that same list (
.IR ": x(val)" ,
for a plain
.IR int / float /etc.,
or a pointer/reference), with real C++'s own declaration-order
semantics \(em a field initializes in the order it's
.I declared
in the class, not the order it's
.I written
in the list, and an initializer argument referencing another member
by its bare name is rewritten correctly too (
.I ": y(x)"
becomes
.IR "this\->y = this\->x" );
duplicate detection for a member-initializer list (
.I ": x(a), x(b)"
is a reported error, not a silent, arbitrary pick between the two).
.PP
.B dynamic_cast
is accepted too, but honestly, not silently: it parses and transpiles
exactly like the other casts above, but since it doesn't actually
perform the RTTI-backed runtime check real
.B dynamic_cast
promises (this project has never supported RTTI, by design), using it
produces a
.B warning ,
not an error \(em printed to stderr, never blocking the transpile.
Warnings are a diagnostic category separate from the semantic errors
described throughout this section \(em they never affect this tool's
own exit status. The other warning, under
.B \-\-target=vircon32
only, flags a class or struct larger than one word passed or returned
by value: the Vircon32 C compiler only accepts one-word parameters and
return values, so pass a pointer instead.
.PP
Not yet supported:
class-typed member-field initializers (
.I ": thing(args)"
where
.IR thing 's
own type is a class, not a primitive/pointer/reference \(em invoking a
member's own constructor is real, separate complexity not supported
anywhere in this project yet); virtual destructor dispatch through
.B delete[]
specifically (plain
.B delete
through a base pointer already dispatches correctly); a real
preprocessor (macro expansion, C++-side visibility of
.BR #define d
names, conditional compilation);
.B static
class members; range-based
.BR for .
.PP
The original list of basic, non-OOP C syntax gaps (confirmed directly
by checking the grammar rather than assumed) is now complete: bitwise
operators,
.BR switch / case ,
bare
.BR struct ,
C-style casts, C++-style casts, hex/octal/binary literals with
suffixes, the ternary operator,
.BR do / while ,
.BR enum ,
.BR union ,
source-level
.BR sizeof ,
.BR goto ,
function pointers, and multi-dimensional arrays are all supported now.
.PP
A fresh audit (confirmed directly, not assumed) found a further,
separate round of common C gaps.
.BR const ,
multiple declarators in one statement, and function-pointer
.BR typedef s
are now closed (see above, and BUGS below for
.BR const 's
exact boundary). Still open:
.BR static ", " extern ", " inline ,
and
.B register
(none recognized at all;
.B volatile
only inside
.BR "asm volatile" );
no adjacent string-literal concatenation
.RI ( "\(dqfoo\(dq \(dqbar\(dq"
does not become
.IR "\(dqfoobar\(dq" );
no bit-fields
.RI ( "unsigned x : 4;"
inside a
.BR struct / union );
no comma operator
.RI ( "a, b, c"
as a single expression). None of these are implemented yet.
.PP
Never supported, by design: templates, exceptions, RTTI, multiple
inheritance.
.SH HEADERS AND C TYPES
.B v32c++
is not a C preprocessor, but it does resolve C++ headers. Before
parsing, every
.B #include
of a file ending in
.I .hpp
or
.I .cpp
is replaced by that file's contents, recursively. Lookup follows the
same order real compilers use (see
.BR \-I ).
A header containing
.B #pragma once
is inlined at most once, and an include cycle without it is an error.
Diagnostics and
.B \-g
debug maps still report the original file and line inside an inlined
header.
.PP
Every other
.B #
line, including every
.I .h
include, is passed through verbatim to the top of the generated C,
where the Vircon32 C compiler handles it. Nothing is macro-expanded,
and
.BR #if / #ifdef
are not evaluated.
.PP
The
.I v32/
directory of the source distribution holds C++ headers for the
Vircon32 C API, one per SDK header
.RI ( video.hpp ", " input.hpp ", " string.hpp ", " time.hpp ,
.IR audio.hpp ", " math.hpp ", " misc.hpp ", " memcard.hpp )
in the
.B v32
namespace. They wrap the SDK's C functions without reimplementing any
hardware access, and each one passes its own
.I .h
include through. What they add: typed enums for the SDK's
.BR #define s,
int/float overload sets
.RB ( v32::minimum ", " v32::clamp ", ...),"
scope-guard classes that restore the console's selected
texture, gamepad, sound or channel at the closing brace, and small
handle classes
.RB ( v32::Channel ", " v32::MemoryCard ", " v32::HeapBlock ", ...)."
Wrapper names never reuse a C function's name, so the raw C API stays
available alongside them.
.PP
A free function declared inside a namespace is emitted under a C name
that includes the namespace
.RI ( v32::draw(int,int,int)
becomes
.IR v32__draw__int_int_int ),
so it can't collide with a function of the same name and parameters
elsewhere. A qualified call
.RI ( v32::draw(...) )
considers only functions in that namespace, and is an error if there
are none. An unqualified call inside a namespace finds that
namespace's functions before those of enclosing scopes.
.PP
An overloaded call whose arguments include an SDK
.B #define
or a C API call result (whose types
.B v32c++
cannot see) still resolves when the remaining arguments' known types
leave exactly one candidate, e.g.
.IR "v32::minimum(rand(), 10)" .
Otherwise it is an error; assign the value to a typed local first.
.PP
Because pass-through headers are never parsed, a C struct type they
define is unknown to
.BR v32c++ .
A
.B native
declaration makes such a name usable:
.PP
.RS
.nf
native date_info;

void stamp( date_info* out )
{
    translate_date( get_date(), out );   // the real C function
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B native
.I Name\fB;
declares
.I Name
as an opaque type and emits no C of its own. It may be used by pointer
or reference anywhere a type may appear: locals, globals, parameters,
return types, class members, casts, and
.IR sizeof(Name*) .
Every by-value use is a semantic error that names the type:
a by-value local, global, parameter, return type, or member
(including a
.B const
one),
.IR sizeof(Name) ,
and member access through a pointer to it. The layout lives in a
header
.B v32c++
never reads, so it can't size the type or check its field names.
Declaring the same native again is harmless. Declaring a name native
when it's already a class, struct, or typedef is an error. A native
declared inside a namespace is still emitted under its bare C name.
.B native
is a reserved word.
.SH CART PACKAGING
Alongside the generated
.IR .c ,
.B v32c++
also writes a Vircon32 cart-packing XML file by default (see
.BR \-x ,
above, to disable this) \(em the
.I rom-definition
Vircon32's own cartridge-packing step expects, so that step no longer
needs a separate, manual, hand-written XML file for every build. Its
own filename is derived the same way the
.I .c
output's own is: the
.I .c
file's extension replaced with
.IR .xml .
.PP
Two
.I cart hint
directives are recognized directly in C++ source, each on its own line:
.PP
.RS
.nf
#texture NAME "file.png"
#sound   NAME "file.wav"
.fi
.RE
.PP
modeled on
.BR v32lua (1)'s
own
.B \-\-#texture
/
.B \-\-#sound
hints. Each
.I NAME
(any case \(em upper, lower, or mixed, never forced to one) becomes a
.B #define
in the generated C, mapping to that resource's id: ids start at 0 and
increase by one per hint, textures and sounds counted separately, in
the order the hints appear in the source \(em the
.I first
.B #texture
is always id 0, the second id 1, and so on; likewise for
.BR #sound .
.I NAME
is then usable anywhere an integer constant would be, as an argument to
whatever texture/sound-selecting function the target program calls.
The same declaration order also determines each resource's position
in the generated XML's
.B <textures>
/
.B <sounds>
sections \(em position
.I is
id, the same invariant
.BR v32lua (1)
itself relies on, so the two always agree. Each filename has its own
extension replaced with
.I .vtex
(textures) or
.I .vsnd
(sounds) in the XML, matching Vircon32's own expected resource formats;
the original extension given in the hint (
.IR .png ", " .wav ,
whatever) only matters for locating the source asset, not for what
ships in the cart.
.PP
A program with no
.B #texture
/
.B #sound
hints at all still gets a generated XML, with empty, self-closing
.B <textures />
/
.B <sounds />
elements \(em the previous, hint-free behavior, unchanged for anything
that doesn't use this.
.PP
Two more hints override the
.B <rom>
element's own
.B title
/
.B version
attributes, each fixed at "Vircon32 Program" / "1.0" (matching
.BR v32lua (1)'s
own defaults) until overridden:
.PP
.RS
.nf
#title "The BIOS/CART title"
#version 1.0
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I title
is quoted (may contain spaces);
.I version
is a bare token, never quoted (matching
.BR v32lua (1)'s
own
.B \-\-#version
hint). If either hint appears more than once, the last one seen wins.
.PP
Not yet supported: a duplicate-name check across
.B #texture
/
.B #sound
hints (two
.B #texture
lines naming the same
.I NAME
currently produce two conflicting
.B #define
lines, caught only later, by the C compiler itself, not by
.B v32c++
directly).
.SH BIOS MODE
.B \-b
(see OPTIONS, above) transpiles a Vircon32
.B BIOS
rather than an ordinary cartridge: the generated XML's
.B <rom>
element gets
.BR type=\(dqbios\(dq
instead of the ordinary
.BR type=\(dqcartridge\(dq ,
and three constraints \(em checked only when
.B \-b
is given, not otherwise \(em must all hold, or
.B v32c++
reports every violation found (not just the first) and exits nonzero
without writing anything:
.IP \(bu 3
Exactly one
.B #texture
hint \(em zero or two-or-more are both errors.
.IP \(bu 3
At most one
.B #sound
hint \(em zero is fine, two-or-more is an error.
.IP \(bu 3
A
.B "void error_handler()"
function, in addition to
.BR main .
.PP
.B v32c++
itself only enforces these three constraints \(em everything else that
makes a valid, bootable Vircon32 BIOS is the C compiler's and
assembler's own concern, downstream of this tool.
.SH DEBUG MAPS
.B \-g
(see OPTIONS, above) writes
.I <output>.debug
alongside the generated C \(em note the full
.I .c.debug
suffix, not a swapped extension (
.I sprite.c
produces
.IR sprite.c.debug ,
not
.IR sprite.debug ).
Each line maps one point in the generated C back to the C++ source
line responsible for it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
c_path,c_line,cpp_path,cpp_line[,function_name]
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I function_name
appears only on the line where a function's own C definition begins,
and names the
.I generated C
side of that function (its mangled name, where this project mangles
one \(em e.g.
.IR Square__area__void ,
not the C++ source's own
.IR area ),
matching which side of the mapping that column actually documents.
.I cpp_path
is always the original
.I .cpp
given on the command line, verbatim \(em never a preprocessed or
otherwise-modified intermediate, regardless of what future tooling
might sit in front of
.B v32c++
one day.
.PP
This is a
.I sparse
table, modeled on a real Vircon32 C-to-assembly debug map: an entry
exists only where the C-line/C++-line association actually changes,
not one row per line of generated C. A lowering-synthesized statement
(a destructor invoked at scope exit, say) still carries some line
number \(em typically the nearest real statement's own, reused \(em so
mappings stay reasonably accurate even for code
.B v32c++
itself generated, not only for what a person directly wrote; this is
not claimed to be exact for every synthesized line.
.SH EXPLANATORY COMMENTS
.B \-vv
(see OPTIONS, above) sprinkles explanatory comments directly into the
generated C, at the points where this project's own C++-to-C
transformation is least obvious to someone reading the output rather
than the original C++ \(em intended to make the generated C worth
reading through on its own, not just a build artifact on the way to a
cartridge. Covers:
.IP \(bu 3
The vtable pointer field in a class's own struct, and where a
struct's own fields stop being its own and start being flattened in
from a base class (C structs have no inheritance of their own).
.IP \(bu 3
The vtable struct type itself, and the single, shared instance every
object of a class points its own vtable field at.
.IP \(bu 3
The explicit
.B this
parameter every method gets (C has no implicit object parameter).
.IP \(bu 3
The allocator/deleter functions
.B new
/
.B delete
lower to, and a virtual destructor's own dispatch through the vtable.
.IP \(bu 3
A destructor invoked automatically at scope exit, a virtual call
dispatched through the vtable, and a
.B new
expression's own call to its allocator \(em each explained at the
specific call site, not just where the underlying function is defined.
.PP
Pure commentary: never changes what code is emitted, only whether a
comment explaining it is emitted alongside it \(em `\-vv`'s own output
is otherwise identical to `\-v`'s. Every comment is detected
structurally, from naming patterns and shapes only this project's own
lowering ever produces (a
.I __dtor__void
mangled suffix; a
.I \->vtable\->
member-access chain; a
.I v32_new_
function-name prefix) \(em nothing C++ source could itself trigger by
coincidence.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I *.cpp
Input source, in the C++ subset this tool accepts.
.TP
.I *.hpp
C++ headers, inlined into the translation unit when
.BR #include d
(see
.BR \-I ).
.TP
.I *.c
Generated Vircon32 C, ready to be handed to the Vircon32 C compiler
directly.
.TP
.I *.xml
Generated cart-packing configuration (see
.B CART PACKAGING
above), ready for Vircon32's own cartridge-packing step. Not written
if
.B \-x
/
.B \-\-no\-xml
was given.
.TP
.I *.c.debug
Generated C-line/C++-line debug map (see
.B DEBUG MAPS
above). Only written if
.B \-g
was given.
.PP
Generated output may reference Vircon32's own standard library
headers (for example
.I misc.h
for
.B malloc
and
.BR free ,
or a video/graphics header for console-specific routines) via
.B #include
lines carried through from the input (or from an inlined
.I .hpp
header) by the preprocessor pass-through \(em these headers are provided by the Vircon32 SDK, not
by
.BR v32c++ ,
and must be available wherever the generated C is actually compiled.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR v32lua (1),
the Vircon32 C compiler documentation.
.PP
