.SH BUGS
.B goto
does not interact correctly with destructors: jumping into or out of a
scope holding a live class-typed local does not invoke that local's
own destructor the way
.BR break / continue / return
already correctly do \(em there is no equivalent pass for
.B goto
yet. There is also no validation that a
.B goto
label actually exists anywhere in its function. Keep
.B goto
to flat scopes with no destructible locals in play until this is
addressed.
.PP
.B const
is only accepted as a PREFIX before a type
.RI ( "const int" ", " "const int *" ,
pointer to const \(em the pointee can't change); a const POINTER
itself
.RI ( "int * const p" ,
the pointer can't be reassigned) is not supported. Most importantly,
this project does not ENFORCE const-correctness anywhere it accepts
the syntax: no error for reassigning a const variable, no error for
writing through
.I this
inside a const member function, no error for calling a non-const
method through a const reference. The syntax is accepted and
correctly emitted in generated C \(em including, for a const member
function, the generated
.I this
parameter's own type \(em with real violations left for the downstream
C/C++ compiler to catch.
.PP
A reference parameter's
.I .member
accesses and its forwarding to another reference parameter lower
correctly, but a
.I scalar
reference
.RI ( int& ", " float& )
read or written directly
.RI ( "y = b; b = 3;" ),
or
.I &ref
of any reference, reads, writes, or takes the address of the
underlying pointer instead. Use an explicit pointer parameter for
primitive out-parameters until this is fixed.
.PP
Two constructs are
.I silently
mistranslated rather than rejected: an in-class default member
initializer
.RI ( "int size = 5;"
on a field declaration) is dropped, and a namespace-scope object
direct-initialized with constructor arguments
.RI ( "Counter g(100);" )
produces an invalid initializer.
.PP
A class declared inside a namespace keeps its bare C name
.RI ( v32::String
is emitted as
.IR String ),
so a program's own class with the same name as a
.B v32
header class collides in the generated C.
.PP
A
.B native
type can be pointed to but not created: v32c++ source has no way to
allocate storage for one, so the pointer must come from C code.
.PP
An unresolvable
.I .h
include (the usual case when the SDK headers aren't next to the
source) is passed through every time it appears, so two headers that
both include it produce the same
.B #include
line twice.
.PP
